---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE, CALENDLY_URL, LINKEDIN_URL, AUTHOR_NAME } from '../consts';

export const prerender = true;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead
			title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
			description="Gerald De Oleo — AEO and GEO for B2B SaaS. Independent consultant, previously product-led growth."
		/>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />

		<main>
			<article class="prose">
				<p class="eyebrow">About</p>
				<h1>{AUTHOR_NAME}</h1>

				<p class="lead">
					I work on answer engine optimization for B2B SaaS — how companies get found, cited, and
					described in AI-generated answers. I work independently, from the Dominican Republic, with
					clients anywhere.
				</p>

				<p>
					Before this I worked on product-led growth for B2B SaaS: the path a buyer takes from first
					hearing about a product to paying for it without ever talking to sales. That path now
					starts somewhere else. The buyer asks a model what to use, and the shortlist is decided
					before anyone lands on your site. Same funnel, different front door.
				</p>

				<p>I've spent the last year on that front door specifically.</p>

				<p>
					Most of what gets called AEO stops at a binary question: did the brand appear, yes or no. I
					think that misses where the failures are. A company can be retrieved, cited, and
					interpreted correctly, and still be described to the buyer in the wrong category.
				</p>

				<p>
					In practice, the problems I find most often aren't hallucinations. They're evidence
					problems. A company pivoted, and most of what's indexed still describes the previous
					product. A directory profile lists competitors from an adjacent category. Two live domains
					split two years of citations. The model isn't wrong — it's reasoning correctly from stale
					material. I wrote up how I separate those cases in
					<a href="/framework">the four layers</a>.
				</p>

				<h2>What I've built</h2>

				<p>
					Two tools, because the problem needed measuring before it needed selling.
					<a href="/ai-crawler-check/">Crawler Check</a> requests your page as each AI bot and shows
					what they actually receive, which is often less than what a browser gets.
					<a href="/positioning-check/">Positioning Check</a> pulls your homepage from the Internet
					Archive at four points over three years and matches it against Common Crawl, so you can see
					which version of your company the training data captured.
				</p>

				<p>Both are free, run on any domain, and need no signup.</p>

				<h2>How I work</h2>

				<p>
					I start by running the queries your buyer would actually type, across the models they'd
					actually use, and documenting what comes back. Not a score — the specific pages, the
					specific descriptions, and who currently owns the answers you should own.
				</p>

				<p>
					From there it's four phases: audit, technical architecture, citation alignment, and
					measurement. They run in order, because publishing content on top of an unresolved entity
					doesn't compound.
				</p>

				<h2>Get in touch</h2>

				<p>
					The fastest way to start is a call. I'll have run the tools on your domain beforehand, so
					we begin with findings rather than questions.
				</p>

				<div class="prose-cta">
					<a href={CALENDLY_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-primary">Book a call</a>
					<a href={LINKEDIN_URL} target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="btn-ghost">LinkedIn</a>
				</div>
			</article>
		</main>

		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>
